{"name":"Dash of Curry","hex":"#ca6e5f","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"dash","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of the following symbols: ‒ (figure dash), – (en dash), — (em dash), or ― (horizontal bar).","A hyphen or minus sign."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To run quickly or for a short distance.","To leave or depart."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/dash"},{"term":"curry","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["One of a family of dishes originating from Indian cuisine, flavored by a spiced sauce.","A spiced sauce or relish, especially one flavored with curry powder."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cook or season with curry powder."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/curry"}],"entries":[{"title":"Dash","blocks":[{"text":"The dash is a punctuation mark consisting of a long horizontal line. It is similar in appearance to the hyphen but is longer and sometimes higher from the baseline. The most common versions are the en dash –, generally longer than the hyphen but shorter than the minus sign; the em dash —, longer than either the en dash or the minus sign; and the horizontal bar ―, whose length varies across typefaces but tends to be between those of the en and em dashes."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dash","wikidata":"Q187819"},{"title":"Curry","blocks":[{"text":"Curry is a dish with a spicy sauce, initially in Indian cuisine, then modified by interchange with the Portuguese, followed by the British, and eventually thoroughly internationalised. Many curries are found in the cuisines of countries in Southeast Asia and East Asia."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6f/Taj_Mahal_-_Lamb_Curry_Madras.jpg/330px-Taj_Mahal_-_Lamb_Curry_Madras.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Curry","wikidata":"Q164606"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons